\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{The witness edge as the other column}
\date{2026-11-21}

\p{The free orbit of \ref{fdip-0126} gives invariant functions
of rank #{72} on one edge. The residual orbit gives a preserved
space of rank #{12}. The characteristic polynomial and the
trace #{358} are the statements of \ref{fdip-012P}. The
determinant and the diagonal bound are the statements of
\ref{fdip-0136}, and the bound uses the Rayleigh inequality.
Two orbits may carry different environment constants. Those
constants scale the two sector sums and leave the #{12} by
#{12} matrix unchanged.}

\proof{
\p{\ref{fdip-0126} gives a free orbit of #{72} points and a
second orbit of #{24} points whose stabilizer has order #{3}.
It counts #{6} double cosets of size #{3} and #{6} of size
#{9}. For the free orbit every stabilizer is trivial.
\ref{fdip-0123} identifies the invariant functions with the
functions on the residual quotient of the links. For one edge
and a trivial stabilizer that quotient is the group, which has
#{72} elements. For the residual orbit, \ref{fdip-012D} shows
that the one-edge operator preserves functions constant on
double cosets and that the rank of that space is the number of
double cosets, hence #{12}.}

\p{The polynomial and the trace #{358} are the statements of
\ref{fdip-012P}. The determinant and the bound
#{\lambda\ge125/2} are the statements of \ref{fdip-0136}, and
that bound assumes the Rayleigh inequality. Under the Rayleigh
hypothesis, \ref{fdip-012Y} supplies a simple largest
eigenvalue with an entrywise positive eigenvector, and
\ref{fdip-0137} gives positive products against the constant
function, the double-coset kernel and the stabilizer
indicator. \ref{fdip-0122} allows the two orbits to carry
different site constants. \ref{fdip-013A} multiplies each
sector sum by its own constant and leaves the one-edge matrix
unchanged.}
}
